  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "The paper develops a monetary valuation framework for changes in eudaimonic wellbeing, measured using Ryff's 1989 Scale, to address the lack of economic values for this dimension of wellness. The analysis confirms that changes in eudaimonic wellbeing carry a higher monetized value than changes in hedonic life satisfaction due to its integration of broader societal connections.",
      "why": "It provides a novel monetization method that allows transport planners to quantify and incorporate long-term, local quality-of-life benefits—such as those from walking and placemaking projects—which are typically undervalued or omitted in traditional cost-benefit appraisals."
    }
